= Wave 0 context audit =

'''State: Phase 3 — Context audit.'''

This page audits Wave 0 after context capture and context compilation/writing. It is not a new research pass and should not introduce substantive claims unless required to correct a discovered defect.

== Audited concepts ==

* [[Algorithm]]
* [[Causality]]
* [[Computation]]
* [[Data structure]]
* [[Hypothesis]]
* [[Program (computing)]]
* [[Programming language]]
* [[Software]]
* [[System boundary]]
* [[Version control]]

Each concept has three reader surfaces:

* Core: <code>Title</code>
* Simple: <code>Title/Simple</code>
* Technical: <code>Title/Technical</code>

Total Wave 0 reader surfaces: '''30'''.

== Structural audit ==

All ten Core pages:

* use [[Template:Knowledge object]];
* use [[Template:Reading depth]];
* declare source status;
* retain at least one domain category;
* contain a source/reference section;
* link to neighboring concepts where the relation contributes to understanding.

All twenty Simple/Technical derivatives:

* use [[Template:Reading depth]];
* declare source status;
* contain a source/reference section;
* intentionally do '''not''' duplicate the Core page's <code>Knowledge object</code> identity;
* intentionally do '''not''' duplicate the Core page's domain category membership.

This keeps one semantic identity per concept while preserving separately addressable reader-depth surfaces.

== Depth consistency audit ==

The following shared distinctions are preserved across Simple/Core/Technical:

<pre>
algorithm ≠ program ≠ computation
program ≠ process
program ≠ software
programming language ≠ implementation/runtime
data structure ≠ abstract data type
hypothesis ≠ prediction ≠ scientific theory
scientific hypothesis ≠ statistical hypothesis
causality ≠ correlation
system boundary ≠ interface ≠ necessarily physical edge
version control ≠ backup ≠ full configuration management
</pre>

Simple pages reduce prerequisites and vocabulary without changing those distinctions.

Technical pages add formalism, implementation constraints, standards, edge cases, or competing models rather than replacing the Core definition.

== Hyperlink audit ==

The Wave 0 graph uses ordinary MediaWiki links for reader navigation and reserves SMW relationships for stronger machine-readable assertions.

Depth-aware convention:

* Core → Core
* Simple → Simple when available, otherwise Core
* Technical → Technical when available, otherwise Core

Redlinks to normalized future concepts are retained where they provide honest coverage signals, including subjects such as [[Evidence]], [[Scientific method]], [[Computability]], [[Computational complexity]], [[Abstract data type]], [[Correlation]], [[Intervention]], [[Counterfactual]], [[Provenance]], and [[Configuration management]].

Redlinks are not treated as permission to generate stubs.

