Direct Scholarpedia coverage for these ten broad Wave 0 concepts was limited; irrelevant same-word matches were not counted as coverage.

== Audit findings and corrections ==

=== 1. Reading-depth identity versus validator ===

'''Finding:''' the existing validator required every Main-space file to belong to a category. That would force <code>/Simple</code> and <code>/Technical</code> derivatives to appear as independent domain members.

'''Correction:''' <code>scripts/validate_v2.py</code> now recognizes Simple/Technical pages using [[Template:Reading depth]] and exempts those derivatives from duplicate category requirements. Core retains canonical identity/category membership.

=== 2. Domain exemplar roles ===

'''Finding:''' the initial rewrite accidentally removed the historical <code>Domain exemplars</code> category from five Wave 0 pages.

'''Correction:''' exemplar role was restored to [[Algorithm]], [[Causality]], [[Hypothesis]], [[System boundary]], and [[Version control]]. Being an exemplar is an architectural role and does not imply that the article must remain small.

=== 3. Research category ===

'''Finding:''' the new capture/compile state documents referenced <code>Category:BITwiki research</code> before the category existed.

'''Correction:''' [[Category:BITwiki research]] was created as a project-facing category for research-state and audit artifacts.

=== 4. Hypothesis technical sourcing ===

'''Finding:''' the statistical-hypothesis and underdetermination sections were broader than the initial reference list.

'''Correction:''' direct NIST statistical-testing and SEP Duhem/underdetermination references were added.

=== 5. Redundant self-link ===

'''Finding:''' <code>System boundary/Technical</code> contained a redundant depth self-link.

'''Correction:''' converted to ordinary prose.

=== 6. Substrate directory semantics ===

'''Finding:''' the substrate audit originally treated each <code>Main/Title/</code> directory as an architectural module and required a local README. Reader-depth directories exist only because repository paths project MediaWiki slash titles; ten READMEs would add transport noise without describing a distinct architecture.

'''Correction:''' <code>scripts/audit_substrate.py</code> now recognizes a depth directory only when a sibling Core page exists, its files are limited to approved Simple/Technical filenames, each derivative actually uses [[Template:Reading depth]], and no nested directory is present. Other undocumented directories remain errors.

=== 7. Deployment/staging title mapping ===

'''Finding:''' slash-depth titles require confirmation that filesystem paths map to the intended MediaWiki titles.

'''Verified:'''

<pre>
Main/Algorithm.mediawiki            → Algorithm
Main/Algorithm/Simple.mediawiki     → Algorithm/Simple
Main/Algorithm/Technical.mediawiki  → Algorithm/Technical
</pre>

Both <code>scripts/deployment_plan.py</code> and <code>staging/compiler/build.py</code> preserve the slash title.

== Domain exemplar preservation ==

Wave 0 Core pages retain the following exemplar roles:

* Computer science → [[Algorithm]]
* Philosophy → [[Causality]]
* Science → [[Hypothesis]]
* Systems science → [[System boundary]]
* Technology → [[Version control]]

The existing exemplars in Biology, Chemistry, Electronics, Energy, Engineering, Mathematics, Medicine, and Physics remain outside this wave.

== Audit acceptance criteria ==

Wave 0 is ready to leave Phase 3 when:

# all 30 reader surfaces remain present;
# one concept = one Core semantic identity;
# Simple/Core/Technical definitions do not materially contradict;
# source roles are traceable and appropriate to the claim type;
# normalized cross-links and depth navigation resolve as designed;
# deployment maps slash paths correctly;
# <code>validate_v2.py</code> passes;
# runtime-schema, substrate, workflow, deployment-plan, and Main-space audits pass;
# the staging compiler builds the complete projection.

GitHub PR checks are the executable evidence for the final criteria. The draft review surface for this wave is PR #16.

